The user asks to predict whether a drug with SMILES "Cc1cc(N(C)C)ccc1C[C@H](C)N" is a substrate of CYP2D6 or not. We need to make a prediction based on molecular properties. We can use available tools to compute various molecular descriptors that might be relevant for CYP2D6 substrate prediction. CYP2D6 substrates tend to have certain characteristics: basic nitrogen, lipophilic, aromatic rings, specific size, etc. We can compute properties like logP, molecular weight, TPSA, HBD, HBA, rotatable bonds, fraction Csp3, etc. Then based on known substrate profiles, we can make an educated guess.

Let's first parse the SMILES: "Cc1cc(N(C)C)ccc1C[C@H](C)N". This appears to be a substituted benzene with a dimethylamino group (N(C)C), a methyl group (Cc1...), and a chain C[C@H](C)N which is a chiral center with an amino group. So it's likely a basic amine compound.

CYP2D6 substrates often have a basic nitrogen atom at a distance of 5-7 Å from the site of metabolism. The molecule has two nitrogen atoms: one tertiary amine (dimethylamino) and one primary amine. Both are basic. The molecule is relatively small.

Let's compute some properties. We'll start with molecular weight, logP, TPSA, HBD, HBA, rotatable bonds, etc. Use tools.
